diagnosis_text:
Current status is healthy and end-to-end connectivity is restored. From pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) ping succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. On the routing side, router1’s RIB contains a BGP-learned route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0, and router2’s RIB contains a BGP-learned route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. BGP on both routers is established and exchanging the expected single prefix in each direction: router1 “show ip bgp summary” shows neighbor 193.10.11.2 in State/PfxRcd=1 (PfxSnt=2), and router2 shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 with PfxRcd=1 (PfxSnt=2). Both sessions have been up for about 1 minute 38–39 seconds at the time of inspection, indicating a very recent re-establishment.

Mechanism and localization: If “their addresses go missing,” it typically means BGP stopped advertising or receiving the partner’s prefix. At present, router1 (AS 1) is learning 200.1.1.0/24 from router2 (AS 2) across the interconnect 193.10.11.0/24, and forwarding works. The brief Up/Down timers strongly suggest the session had been down or reset shortly before this check.

Root cause: No ongoing fault; BGP appears to have recently flapped and then recovered.

Corrective fix: None required now. If this recurs, enable/collect FRR BGP logs and verify session stability (interface/link health and BGP keepalive/hold timers). Consider monitoring alerts on the router1–router2 peering to catch future flaps quickly.
